# Resource CQ #1 (Section 3): "Which edge clusters have the highest
# computational capabilities (in terms of GPU and CPU resources) for
# executing computationally intensive applications?"
#
# UPDATED 2026-08-25: previously an empty template (the paper does not
# name specific GPU/CPU properties and FILL's own data does not carry
# them). Now backed by real data: extensions/intend-telenor-extension.ttl
# defines telenor:gpuCount/telenor:cpuCount, populated in
# examples/telenor-network-data.ttl from a real Telenor edge-cluster KG
# (40 clusters, Denmark/Finland/Sweden/Norway).
#
# Filters on telenor:EdgeCluster rather than intend:BusinessResource:
# individuals are asserted with the specific type only (a telenor:
# EdgeCluster, itself rdfs:subClassOf intend:BusinessResource), and plain
# SPARQL basic graph pattern matching does not walk rdfs:subClassOf
# unless the engine performs RDFS/OWL entailment first (scripts/
# validate.py's --queries mode does not) — same root cause already noted
# in queries/05-user-owned-machines.rq.
PREFIX telenor: <https://w3id.org/intend/extensions/telenor#>
PREFIX rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>

SELECT ?resource ?name ?gpuCount ?cpuCount WHERE {
    ?resource a telenor:EdgeCluster ;
              rdfs:label ?name ;
              telenor:gpuCount ?gpuCount ;
              telenor:cpuCount ?cpuCount .
}
ORDER BY DESC(?gpuCount) DESC(?cpuCount)
